Analysis of US Public Transit Agency's Light and Heavy Rail on Ridership (2002–2026)

1. Research Problem
Objective - Investigate the relationship between transit service supply (measured in Vehicle Revenue Miles - VRM) and ridership (measured in Unlinked Passenger Trips - UPT) across the top 10 major U.S. transit agencies.

2. Data Source and License
Source - National Transit Database (NTD) monthly time-series dataset (2002–2026). https://data.transportation.gov/Public-Transit/Complete-Monthly-Ridership-with-Adjustments-and-Es/8bui-9xvu/about_data
Scope - Restricted to the top 10 transit agencies by ridership volume, filtering specifically for Heavy Rail (HR) and Light Rail (LR) operating modes.
License/Usage: Publicly available federal transportation data provided via the U.S. Department of Transportation / Federal Transit Administration (FTA).

3. Data Cleaning & Preparation Log
1. Importing & Type Conversion - Master data was loaded, and raw text-formatted metrics (UPT and VRM) containing commas were converted into non-destructive numeric columns (UPT_num and VRM_num) while preserving original strings.
2. Filtering Scope - Filtered down to the top 10 agencies and focused strictly on rail modes (HR and LR).
3. Handling Missing Values - Applied `.dropna(subset=['UPT_num', 'VRM_num'])` to eliminate missing reporting months, resulting in a clean analysis-ready dataset of 3,968 monthly records.
4. Discrepancy Note - Noted structural variations in time-series lengths (e.g., New Jersey Transit operating multiple sub-modes yielding ~592 records) due to how regional networks report separate operating divisions to the NTD.

4. Findings & Visualizations (EDA)
   1. Monthly Ridership Trends (Time Series) 
   Heavy Rail overwhelmingly dominates baseline volume compared to Light Rail. Both modes display a sharp, unprecedented drop during the 2020 pandemic lockdowns followed by a steady recovery curve.

   2. Service Supply vs. Passenger Demand (Scatter Plot) 
   Displays a tight, powerful positive correlation. Heavy rail systems cluster heavily in the upper-right quadrant with high mileage and massive ridership counts.

   3. Operational Efficiency (Passenger Trips per Revenue Mile) 
   Compares passenger density relative to service miles run, highlighting how specific light rail networks achieve high passenger yields per mile operated.

5. Predictive Linear Regression Model
Model Type - Ordinary Least Squares Linear Regression (`scikit-learn`).
Results
  * R^2 Score 0.9140 (91.4% of the variance in ridership is explained by vehicle revenue miles supplied).
  Slope (Coefficient) 6.6042 (~6.6 new passenger boardings generated per additional vehicle revenue mile operated).
  Intercept - `-4,063,781` (A mathematical artifact representing model extrapolation limits near zero).


6. Limitations, Ethics, and Model Bias
Pandemic Distortion - Long-term linear trends are heavily influenced by the structural shock of the 2020–2021 COVID-19 pandemic and subsequent shifting hybrid-work commuting patterns.
Reporting Inconsistencies - Variations in how local agencies count and report boardings (turnstile counts vs. automated passenger counters) introduce minor reporting discrepancies.
Service Supply vs. Equity - High statistical efficiency (`VRM` to `UPT` ratio) does not automatically equal equitable transit access. Agencies must balance high-demand trunk lines with essential coverage routes in lower-density neighborhoods.


